6,61.3 350.4,61.2 351.2,61.2 351.9,61.1 352.7,61 353.5,61 354.2,60.9 355,60.8 355.8,60.8 356.5,60.7 357.3,60.6 358.1,60.6 358.9,60.5 359.6,60.5 360.4,60.4 361.2,60.3 361.9,60.3 362.7,60.2 363.5,60.1 364.3,60.1 365,60 365.8,60 366.6,59.9 367.3,59.9 368.1,59.8 368.9,59.7 369.7,59.7 370.4,59.6 371.2,59.6 372,59.5 372.7,59.4 373.5,59.4 374.3,59.3 375.1,59.3 375.8,59.2 376.6,59.2 377.4,59.1 378.1,59.1 378.9,59 379.7,58.9 380.5,58.9 381.2,58.8 382,58.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polyline points="82.3,139.7 241,60.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/></svg>', 'Graph of gravitational potential against distance from the planet''s centre, with a dashed tangent at r = 1.5 × 10⁷ m of gradient 1.8 J kg⁻¹ m⁻¹.'),
('e7ad6c7f-bcbc-5b40-88c5-cf3228cc2c0d', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 42, 42, 'A student claims that the gravitational potential halfway between two equal stars is zero because the fields cancel there. What is wrong?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The fields cancel but the potentials add: the potential is twice the (negative) potential due to one star', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Field strength is a vector and cancels at the midpoint. Potential is a scalar: −GM/d − GM/d = −2GM/d, which is not zero. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: field vectors cancel. C1: potentials are scalars and add. A1: C (The fields cancel but the potentials add: the potential is twice the (negative) potential due to one star).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('0609e4b7-6d78-59d7-904e-dd79503da6f4', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 43, 43, 'The gravitational potential at the surface of an asteroid of radius 3.0 km is −24 J kg⁻¹. Calculate the mass of the asteroid. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.079e15', 'kg', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'φ = −GM/R, so M = −φR/G = 24 × 3000 ÷ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ = 1.08 × 10¹⁵ kg. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: φ = −GM/R. C1: rearranged with R in m. A1: M = 1.08 × 10¹⁵ kg.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('a7025fee-c382-556f-865a-2d822e59f86a', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 44, 44, 'The gravitational potential at a height h above a planet is −4.0 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹ and at the surface it is −6.0 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹. A 3.0 kg mass falls from rest from height h to the surface (no air). Calculate its kinetic energy on arrival.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6e7', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Ek = mΔφ = 3.0 × (−4.0 × 10⁷ − (−6.0 × 10⁷)) = 6.0 × 10⁷ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: loss of Ep = mΔφ. C1: Ek gained = Ep lost. A1: Ek = 6.0 × 10⁷ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cb7390b0-aadf-593d-bcd0-74527a8272b7', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 45, 45, 'Which expression gives the gravitational potential energy of a mass m at a point where the gravitational potential is φ?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'mφ', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'φ is energy per unit mass, so Ep = mφ (negative, because φ is negative). Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: φ defined per unit mass. C1: Ep = mφ. A1: A (mφ).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('b43cc65b-9e1b-54d3-92d1-b9f963bdc02b', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 46, 46, 'A 2500 kg communications satellite is launched from the Earth''s surface into a geostationary orbit of radius 4.22 × 10⁷ m. (a) Calculate the gravitational potential at the surface and at the orbit. (b) Calculate the increase in the satellite''s gravitational potential energy. (c) Explain why the launch must supply more energy than this. Give only the answer to (b). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.327e11', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) φ_surface = −3.98 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 6.37 × 10⁶ = −6.25 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹, φ_orbit = −3.98 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 4.22 × 10⁷ = −9.44 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹. (b) ΔEp = mΔφ = 2500 × (−9.436 × 10⁶ − (−6.251 × 10⁷)) = 1.33 × 10¹¹ J. (c) The satellite also needs kinetic energy to orbit at 3.07 km s⁻¹, and energy is wasted lifting fuel and against air resistance. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: φ = −GM/r. A1: both potentials. C1: ΔEp = mΔφ. A1: ΔEp = 1.33 × 10¹¹ J. B1: kinetic energy and losses.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1d7a1537-d168-5b72-8d55-e2142dd9e324', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 47, 47, 'Equipotentials around a planet are drawn at −8.0, −6.0, −4.0 and −2.0 MJ kg⁻¹. (a) Explain why the equipotentials are spheres that get further apart moving outwards, although the potential steps are equal. (b) The −8.0 and −6.0 MJ kg⁻¹ equipotentials have radii 5.0 × 10⁶ m and 6.67 × 10⁶ m. Estimate the average field strength between them. (c) Calculate GM from the −8.0 MJ kg⁻¹ surface. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.198', 'N kg⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) φ ∝ −1/r: equal steps in φ need larger steps in r as r grows (the field weakens). (b) g ≈ Δφ/Δr = 2.0 × 10⁶ ÷ (6.67 × 10⁶ − 5.0 × 10⁶) = 1.20 N kg⁻¹. (c) GM = −φr = 8.0 × 10⁶ × 5.0 × 10⁶ = 4.0 × 10¹³ N m² kg⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: φ ∝ −1/r. B1: field weakens so spacing increases. C1: g = Δφ/Δr. A1: g. C1: GM = −φr. A1: g = 1.20 N kg⁻¹.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="fge91b7777-t fge91b7777-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fge91b7777-t">Equipotentials</title><desc id="fge91b7777-d">Equipotential surfaces around a planet at −8.0, −6.0, −4.0 and −2.0 MJ kg⁻¹, drawn with radii proportional to 1/|φ|, so they get further apart going outwards.</desc><circle cx="170" cy="170" r="21.9" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="170" cy="170" r="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="271.3" y1="64.9" x2="330" y2="64.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="271.3" cy="64.9" r="2.4" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="334" y="69.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−2.0 MJ kg⁻¹</text><circle cx="170" cy="170" r="73" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="220.7" y1="117.4" x2="330" y2="117.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="220.7" cy="117.4" r="2.4" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="334" y="122.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−4.0</text><circle cx="170" cy="170" r="48.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="210.8" y1="143.4" x2="330" y2="143.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="210.8" cy="143.4" r="2.4" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="334" y="148.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−6.0</text><circle cx="170" cy="170" r="36.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="206.5" y1="169.4" x2="330" y2="169.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="206.5" cy="169.4" r="2.4" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="334" y="174.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−8.0</text></svg>', 'Equipotential surfaces around a planet at −8.0, −6.0, −4.0 and −2.0 MJ kg⁻¹, drawn with radii proportional to 1/|φ|, so they get further apart going outwards.'),
('54480ad8-6464-5c89-9e7f-6f51794c3959', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 48, 48, 'A spacecraft is at rest at the point between the Earth and Moon where the resultant field is zero, 3.46 × 10⁸ m from the Earth''s centre (3.8 × 10⁷ m from the Moon''s). Moon''s mass = 7.35 × 10²² kg, radius 1.74 × 10⁶ m. (a) Calculate the potential at this point. (b) Calculate the potential at the Moon''s surface (include the Earth''s contribution, taking the Earth–Moon distance as 3.84 × 10⁸ m). (c) Calculate the speed with which the spacecraft would reach the Moon''s surface if given a tiny push towards the Moon. Give only the answer to (c). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2269', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) φ = −3.98 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 3.46 × 10⁸ − 4.90 × 10¹² ÷ 3.8 × 10⁷ = −1.28 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹. (b) φ = −3.98 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸ − 4.90 × 10¹² ÷ 1.74 × 10⁶ = −3.85 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹. (c) ½v² = Δφ: v = √(2 × (−1.280 × 10⁶ − (−3.854 × 10⁶))) = 2270 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: potentials add. A1: φ at neutral point. A1: φ at Moon surface. C1: ½v² = Δφ. A1: v = 2270 m s⁻¹.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 207.1" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg4bdef203-t fg4bdef203-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg4bdef203-t">Neutral point</title><desc id="fg4bdef203-d">The spacecraft at the point between the Earth and the Moon where the resultant field is zero: 3.46 × 10⁸ m from the Earth''s centre and 3.8 × 10⁷ m from the Moon''s.</desc><polygon points="70,149.1 76.3,146.2 76.3,152.1" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="74.9" y1="149.1" x2="317.4" y2="149.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="322.3,149.1 315.9,152.1 315.9,146.2" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="196.1" y="161.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3.46 × 10⁸ m</text><polygon points="322.3,58 328.6,55 328.6,61" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="327.2" y1="58" x2="345.1" y2="58" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="350,58 343.7,61 343.7,55" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="336.1" y="46" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3.8 × 10⁷ m</text><circle cx="70" cy="98.1" r="22" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="70" y="64.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Earth</text><circle cx="350" cy="98.1" r="8" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="350" y="78.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Moon</text><circle cx="322.3" cy="98.1" r="3.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="322.3" y="118.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">spacecraft</text></svg>', 'The spacecraft at the point between the Earth and the Moon where the resultant field is zero: 3.46 × 10⁸ m from the Earth''s centre and 3.8 × 10⁷ m from the Moon''s.'),
('3507f3e2-f4a2-59f4-8a2b-0596abbe2c68', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 49, 49, 'A student plots φ against 1/r for a planet using: 1/r = 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ m⁻¹, φ = −3.0 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹; 1/r = 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m⁻¹, φ = −6.0 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹; 1/r = 3.0 × 10⁻⁷ m⁻¹, φ = −9.0 × 10⁷ J kg⁻¹. (a) Describe the graph. (b) Determine GM from it. (c) Calculate the planet''s mass. Give only the answer to (c). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.498e24', 'kg', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) A straight line through the origin with negative gradient: φ ∝ −1/r. (b) gradient = −3.0 × 10⁷ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ = −3.0 × 10¹⁴ J m kg⁻¹ = −GM, so GM = 3.0 × 10¹⁴ N m² kg⁻¹. (c) M = 3.0 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ = 4.50 × 10²⁴ kg. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: straight line through origin, negative gradient. C1: gradient = −GM. A1: GM. C1: M = GM/G. A1: M = 4.50 × 10²⁴ kg.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg73c47597-t fg73c47597-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg73c47597-t">φ against 1/r</title><desc id="fg73c47597-d">Graph of gravitational potential against 1/r: the points lie on a straight line through the origin with negative gradient.</desc><line x1="36.2" y1="34" x2="36.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="122.6" y1="34" x2="122.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="209.1" y1="34" x2="209.1" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="295.5" y1="34" x2="295.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="242" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="200.4" x2="382" y2="200.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="158.8" x2="382" y2="158.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="117.2" x2="382" y2="117.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="75.6" x2="382" y2="75.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="34" x2="382" y2="34" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="34" x2="388" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="242" x2="36.2" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,34 385.7,37 385.7,31" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="36.2,24 39.1,30.3 33.2,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="36.2" y1="34" x2="36.2" y2="38" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="36.2" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="122.6" y1="34" x2="122.6" y2="38" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="122.6" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1</text><line x1="209.1" y1="34" x2="209.1" y2="38" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="209.1" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2</text><line x1="295.5" y1="34" x2="295.5" y2="38" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="295.5" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3</text><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="38" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="382" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4</text><line x1="32.2" y1="242" x2="36.2" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="242" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−10</text><line x1="32.2" y1="200.4" x2="36.2" y2="200.4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="200.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−8</text><line x1="32.2" y1="158.8" x2="36.2" y2="158.8" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="158.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−6</text><line x1="32.2" y1="117.2" x2="36.2" y2="117.2" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="117.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−4</text><line x1="32.2" y1="75.6" x2="36.2" y2="75.6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="75.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−2</text><line x1="32.2" y1="34" x2="36.2" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.2" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><text x="209.1" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">(1/r) / 10⁻⁷ m⁻¹</text><text x="32.2" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">φ / 10⁷ J kg⁻¹</text><line x1="118.6" y1="92.4" x2="126.6" y2="100.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="118.6" y1="100.4" x2="126.6" y2="92.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.1" y1="154.8" x2="213.1" y2="162.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.1" y1="162.8" x2="213.1" y2="154.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="291.5" y1="217.2" x2="299.5" y2="225.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="291.5" y1="225.2" x2="299.5" y2="217.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'Graph of gravitational potential against 1/r: the points lie on a straight line through the origin with negative gradient.'),
('272f112b-d507-537e-b29b-28802065934b', 'd70c2ad3-65ef-4eb3-a897-6802ce9a60c1', 'd22251cb-cf0a-5cdb-a0a6-bea9552467ec', 'a-level', '17.4', '9702 coursebook 17.4 · Gravitational potential', 50, 50, 'Two stars, each of mass 3.0 × 10³⁰ kg, are 6.0 × 10¹¹ m apart. (a) Calculate the gravitational potential at the midpoint. (b) State the field strength at the midpoint and explain the difference in behaviour of φ and g. (c) Calculate the minimum energy a 1000 kg probe at the midpoint needs to escape from both stars. Give only the answer to (c). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.334e12', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) φ = −2GM/(d/2) = −2 × 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 3.0 × 10³⁰ ÷ 3.0 × 10¹¹ = −1.33 × 10⁹ J kg⁻¹. (b) g = 0: the vector fields cancel, but potential is a scalar and the contributions add. (c) E = m × |φ| = 1000 × 1.33 × 10⁹ = 1.33 × 10¹² J. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: distance d/2 from each. A1: φ. B1: g = 0. B1: scalar vs vector. C1: E = m|φ|. A1: E = 1.33 × 10¹² J.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 146.7" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg668a2ef7-t fg668a2ef7-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg668a2ef7-t">Two equal stars</title><desc id="fg668a2ef7-d">Two stars, each of mass 3.0 × 10³⁰ kg, 6.0 × 10¹¹ m apart, with M the midpoint between them.</desc><polygon points="70,96.7 76.3,93.7 76.3,99.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="74.9" y1="96.7" x2="345.1" y2="96.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="350,96.7 343.7,99.6 343.7,93.7" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="210" y="108.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">6.0 × 10¹¹ m</text><circle cx="70" cy="50" r="14" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="70" y="24" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">star</text><circle cx="350" cy="50" r="14" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="350" y="24" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">star</text><circle cx="210" cy="50" r="3.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="218" y="38" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">M</text></svg>', 'Two stars, each of mass 3.0 × 10³⁰ kg, 6.0 × 10¹¹ m apart, with M the midpoint between them.')
INSERT INTO problem_options (id, problem_id, option_text, option_letter, is_correct, "order") VALUES
('379163a3-36ba-538e-9f19-63e242f54b66', '9ee9e0f9-06b8-5fa5-a775-2c6de579ad1d', 'The work done in bringing a mass from the surface to that point', 'A', false, 0),
('c5177049-a4e2-5ef0-8574-12dcf49401e3', '9ee9e0f9-06b8-5fa5-a775-2c6de579ad1d', 'The force per unit mass at that point', 'B', false, 1),
('b0d19256-6520-56b1-8e7c-a55ccb9fcb54', '9ee9e0f9-06b8-5fa5-a775-2c6de579ad1d', 'The work done per unit mass in bringing a small test mass from infinity to that point', 'C', true, 2),
('16cb295a-ad40-5d5b-a897-92be20c4888a', '9ee9e0f9-06b8-5fa5-a775-2c6de579ad1d', 'The energy of a 1 kg mass at the surface of the Earth', 'D', false, 3),
('bc1540af-d591-57a0-b3bb-9044949a072c', '5f1ff724-7a0f-58c6-bbe6-6e932852fe23', 'J kg⁻¹', 'A', true, 0),
('42d06f32-6027-5379-a7a0-098e31b3863a', '5f1ff724-7a0f-58c6-bbe6-6e932852fe23', 'N kg⁻¹', 'B', false, 1),
('8c31adb0-67f9-559f-815f-31bcd821f97b', '5f1ff724-7a0f-58c6-bbe6-6e932852fe23', 'J', 'C', false, 2),
('7ae7f8a8-fc20-50a9-b1ca-6930928b1cc3', '5f1ff724-7a0f-58c6-bbe6-6e932852fe23', 'N m kg', 'D', false, 3),
('addc9ebe-6c64-5eba-8372-fbb9e8d462f4', 'e1ad5319-4bb5-5ae8-a1c8-55198599c1e3', 'It decreases towards zero', 'A', false, 0),
('2739ae8c-e1cf-58be-bf5e-66b47dd94b28', 'e1ad5319-4bb5-5ae8-a1c8-55198599c1e3', 'It increases to a positive maximum', 'B', false, 1),
('d2c1410d-10a4-5708-a1fc-4611b0a1a970', 'e1ad5319-4bb5-5ae8-a1c8-55198599c1e3', 'It stays constant', 'C', false, 2),
('fe1c7d8f-7cc1-5e6a-92f6-a95e1f3dad8c', 'e1ad5319-4bb5-5ae8-a1c8-55198599c1e3', 'It increases from a negative value towards zero', 'D', true, 3),
('251517aa-2943-53bc-95c7-f0772993f802', '79c9e6ff-b558-5b67-ba81-4f36458dbabe', 'A surface where the field strength is zero', 'A', false, 0),
('3a36dace-9f6c-5946-b476-df62280c9350', '79c9e6ff-b558-5b67-ba81-4f36458dbabe', 'A surface on which every point has the same gravitational potential', 'B', true, 1),
('e7d46f36-b641-5d2d-851c-2e1265705efd', '79c9e6ff-b558-5b67-ba81-4f36458dbabe', 'A surface along which a mass accelerates freely', 'C', false, 2),
('0758b3e7-27db-5186-a5a2-c5463f48aa00', '79c9e6ff-b558-5b67-ba81-4f36458dbabe', 'A surface perpendicular to the Earth''s axis', 'D', false, 3),
('c98b3d67-f2fb-5f0b-8d22-b22971c32158', 'e2352474-56a7-5e1a-8e6c-6d7cea37e62b', 'They are always parallel', 'A', false, 0),
('cb332b6c-68c5-5f53-8502-2442cb8b68ad', 'e2352474-56a7-5e1a-8e6c-6d7cea37e62b', 'They meet at 45°', 'B', false, 1),
('6c6339a3-2d3e-59f3-8e30-07235b922800', 'e2352474-56a7-5e1a-8e6c-6d7cea37e62b', 'They are always perpendicular', 'C', true, 2),
('fd0fef0e-d18f-5a63-bd46-0e368a0ca461', 'e2352474-56a7-5e1a-8e6c-6d7cea37e62b', 'Field lines lie along the equipotentials', 'D', false, 3),
('7095fbb1-3c2f-591a-a055-69f7181838ba', 'decadf2d-b4ef-54c1-b4d4-bbad57b4ecbf', 'φ/3', 'A', true, 0),
('a7ebb0c8-8cd6-55c9-a49a-3b0660782ea0', 'decadf2d-b4ef-54c1-b4d4-bbad57b4ecbf', '3φ', 'B', false, 1),
('48828ad9-7d6a-56b4-b6d1-d2ba5be721fc', 'decadf2d-b4ef-54c1-b4d4-bbad57b4ecbf', 'φ/9', 'C', false, 2),
('858b5640-295b-5b82-8085-73d8c51e89c7', 'decadf2d-b4ef-54c1-b4d4-bbad57b4ecbf', '9φ', 'D', false, 3),
('ffb61566-0a33-5cf7-bb92-24a0bc0a2d95', '9645d7bf-a26a-5e26-b0f1-6272d71b6308', 'They must be added as vectors', 'A', false, 0),
('5dfee2c2-3fb2-52b4-b463-0869ac44be30', '9645d7bf-a26a-5e26-b0f1-6272d71b6308', 'Only the larger one counts', 'B', false, 1),
('88ad2205-541b-5cfa-9b68-5572036c420e', '9645d7bf-a26a-5e26-b0f1-6272d71b6308', 'They cancel if the planets are on opposite sides', 'C', false, 2),
('099a621c-2709-5710-838e-042b1a276460', '9645d7bf-a26a-5e26-b0f1-6272d71b6308', 'The potentials simply add as numbers', 'D', true, 3),
('52c809e4-69e5-55ee-8859-42b2b4e3d1c6', '15c65c28-08f9-5e85-b328-a92165cde985', 'The potential at the point', 'A', false, 0),
('96e3010f-f20e-5bfb-89f8-928f16be2f58', '15c65c28-08f9-5e85-b328-a92165cde985', 'Minus the potential gradient, −dφ/dr', 'B', true, 1),
('70c03d07-a699-57c3-8ac9-6350a994d8e8', '15c65c28-08f9-5e85-b328-a92165cde985', 'The potential divided by the distance', 'C', false, 2),
('544409a9-f4c5-589a-af98-c2b6543f0457', '15c65c28-08f9-5e85-b328-a92165cde985', 'The area under the φ–r graph', 'D', false, 3),
('a0e88a8b-47c8-53d8-8491-2396159d145e', 'ab9285f3-f19c-5dce-adca-8c899198d974', 'At the Earth''s surface', 'A', false, 0),
('0ecebd44-1853-53c5-8482-8599a99f86f1', 'ab9285f3-f19c-5dce-adca-8c899198d974', 'At the centre of the Earth', 'B', false, 1),
('6f67d1c6-290d-5aa3-aacb-7f85e4df0db3', 'ab9285f3-f19c-5dce-adca-8c899198d974', 'At a point a long way from the Earth', 'C', true, 2),
('5d8eb2ee-cb87-54b9-95d2-8a622d608b83', 'ab9285f3-f19c-5dce-adca-8c899198d974', 'In a low orbit', 'D', false, 3),
('cb5a0efe-f23e-5741-b7e6-648b213a3489', 'a54c3388-702b-5209-8e5f-4bd58d22c51f', '1.0 × 10⁸ J', 'A', true, 0),
('2ab684a0-5678-51fc-9f15-24f7ce461e4f', 'a54c3388-702b-5209-8e5f-4bd58d22c51f', '5.0 × 10⁷ J', 'B', false, 1),
('7fbeb71c-734f-5893-aa94-39d687fb2639', 'a54c3388-702b-5209-8e5f-4bd58d22c51f', '−1.0 × 10⁸ J', 'C', false, 2),
('083a7454-a829-57eb-8db2-1ba49cf1e2cd', 'a54c3388-702b-5209-8e5f-4bd58d22c51f', '2.5 × 10⁷ J', 'D', false, 3),
('ca529f5a-ea8c-5678-bdd2-b8ed8e3af20d', '89326eb9-3aa6-5fd4-8dc3-1c0ec6e6e163', 'A straight line through the origin', 'A', false, 0),
('4a6e7a05-d66c-5c11-a442-2542333ffc38', '89326eb9-3aa6-5fd4-8dc3-1c0ec6e6e163', 'A curve falling from zero to large negative values', 'B', false, 1),
('3be3fd46-d55b-5178-9f63-da60df5fa2de', '89326eb9-3aa6-5fd4-8dc3-1c0ec6e6e163', 'A horizontal line', 'C', false, 2),
('4ff5cdb1-7564-5269-a3a1-978770fde968', '89326eb9-3aa6-5fd4-8dc3-1c0ec6e6e163', 'A curve rising from a large negative value towards zero, getting less steep', 'D', true, 3),
('e020c1eb-f08a-5d53-9c92-9c9760092cd3', '21e631fe-ed1f-5ad7-a1b2-47cdf97b2301', 'Work done depends on the path', 'A', false, 0),
('0abac433-e0b3-559d-b869-35a11b207d89', '21e631fe-ed1f-5ad7-a1b2-47cdf97b2301', 'No net work is done by gravity, whatever path the satellite takes', 'B', true, 1),
('f9eda69c-6b80-5b64-a005-0ea97b573e34', '21e631fe-ed1f-5ad7-a1b2-47cdf97b2301', 'The satellite must stay at the same speed throughout', 'C', false, 2),
('29e87f10-606e-5fe6-bc20-198034e72cd6', '21e631fe-ed1f-5ad7-a1b2-47cdf97b2301', 'The field strength is zero between X and Y', 'D', false, 3),
('b7701cab-75f5-52a9-aea6-bb678281d3d7', 'e7ad6c7f-bcbc-5b40-88c5-cf3228cc2c0d', 'Nothing: zero field means zero potential', 'A', false, 0),
('2923ae3e-1dec-5f9d-aeff-0f4edd38dbd8', 'e7ad6c7f-bcbc-5b40-88c5-cf3228cc2c0d', 'The potential is positive there', 'B', false, 1),
('33d76555-0866-5a4e-a5e5-9b1b2aa9048d', 'e7ad6c7f-bcbc-5b40-88c5-cf3228cc2c0d', 'The fields cancel but the potentials add: the potential is twice the (negative) potential due to one star', 'C', true, 2),
('13b02083-9f97-5dfe-bd70-0eb4418c13b0', 'e7ad6c7f-bcbc-5b40-88c5-cf3228cc2c0d', 'The potentials cancel but the fields add', 'D', false, 3),
('763a4dd6-723e-53f5-96d7-ebdfb4d6d154', 'cb7390b0-aadf-593d-bcd0-74527a8272b7', 'mφ', 'A', true, 0),
('a4d0c85a-6126-56f5-b5da-e1aa52d16d9a', 'cb7390b0-aadf-593d-bcd0-74527a8272b7', 'φ/m', 'B', false, 1),
('34ec4627-96c1-588c-a264-4358c84d0b1c', 'cb7390b0-aadf-593d-bcd0-74527a8272b7', '−mφ', 'C', false, 2),
('40eb019e-f8e0-5c1b-ad7b-a953365a9968', 'cb7390b0-aadf-593d-bcd0-74527a8272b7', 'mφ²', 'D', false, 3)
INSERT INTO problems (id, chapter_id, topic_id, curriculum_id, topic_code, syllabus_cite, problem_number, "order", question_text, question_image_url, difficulty_level, answer_type, answer_correct, answer_unit, answer_unit_required, answer_tolerance, answer_sign_sensitive, answer_alternates, explanation, points, figure_svg, figure_alt) VALUES
('4536f738-dacd-5a3e-8c58-96d0bf468088', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 33, 33, 'A satellite is in a circular orbit around the Earth. Which force provides the centripetal force?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The gravitational attraction of the Earth', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Gravity towards the Earth''s centre is the only significant force and it provides the centripetal force GMm/r² = mv²/r. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The gravitational attraction of the Earth).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('74539402-a8c2-5c8a-8d43-a0cca6c45ff6', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 34, 34, 'For a circular orbit of radius r around a planet of mass M, which expression gives the orbital speed?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '√(GM/r)', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v² = GM/r, so v = √(GM/r). Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (√(GM/r)).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('99baf9a8-019b-530b-8ed6-e61b68f57274', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 35, 35, 'Calculate the orbital speed of a satellite in a circular orbit of radius 8.0 × 10⁶ m around the Earth. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7055', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) = √(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5.97 × 10²⁴ ÷ 8.0 × 10⁶) = 7060 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). A1: v = 7060 m s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('87cec281-8c86-5eb9-9d73-032875736e6a', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 36, 36, 'Two satellites orbit the Earth at the same radius. One has twice the mass of the other. How do their orbital speeds compare?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They are equal', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) does not contain the satellite''s mass. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (They are equal).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('095a28e4-adf1-5e75-b949-8734ffa451ca', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 37, 37, 'A satellite moves to an orbit of larger radius around the same planet. What happens to its orbital speed?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It decreases', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) ∝ 1/√r, so a larger orbit is slower. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (It decreases).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('a7f9b882-1145-5820-a558-7c3c5db7d251', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 38, 38, 'The Moon orbits the Earth at a radius of 3.84 × 10⁸ m. Calculate its orbital speed. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1018', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) = √(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5.97 × 10²⁴ ÷ 3.84 × 10⁸) = 1020 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). A1: v = 1020 m s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('97d2de7f-f738-5878-ada0-8843e92ac2cb', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 39, 39, 'Calculate the speed of the Earth in its orbit around the Sun (orbit radius 1.50 × 10¹¹ m, Sun''s mass 1.99 × 10³⁰ kg). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '29747', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) = √(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 1.99 × 10³⁰ ÷ 1.50 × 10¹¹) = 2.97 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). A1: v = 2.97 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('bc0c6734-7067-5178-9c8c-9db5a6b68e69', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 40, 40, 'Which equation is used to derive the orbital speed of a satellite?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'GMm/r² = mv²/r', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The gravitational force provides the centripetal force, so GMm/r² = mv²/r. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (GMm/r² = mv²/r).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('8dbd497b-a3a0-5ae5-9486-91f6f1171ce8', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 41, 41, 'A probe orbits Venus (mass 4.87 × 10²⁴ kg) at a radius of 7.0 × 10⁶ m. Calculate its orbital speed. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6812', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) = √(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 4.87 × 10²⁴ ÷ 7.0 × 10⁶) = 6810 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). A1: v = 6810 m s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('71d9b9b5-817a-5a7e-8ce3-19dda0426ba1', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 42, 42, 'A satellite''s orbit radius is made four times larger. By what factor does its orbital speed change?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '½', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v ∝ 1/√r, so increasing r by 4 divides v by √4 = 2. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (½).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b9941402-10f4-55ad-8c06-2906cabbf70f', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 43, 43, 'A moon orbits a planet at 2.5 × 10⁸ m with a speed of 1.4 km s⁻¹. Calculate the mass of the planet. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7.346e24', 'kg', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v² = GM/r, so M = v²r/G = 1400² × 2.5 × 10⁸ ÷ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ = 7.35 × 10²⁴ kg. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: v² = GM/r. C1: M = v²r/G. C1: speed in m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('dbd1e988-9a88-56df-bdcc-666afcd87c70', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 44, 44, 'A satellite orbits the Earth at 6.9 km s⁻¹. Calculate the radius of its orbit. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8.364e6', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'r = GM/v² = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 5.97 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6900² = 8.36 × 10⁶ m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: v² = GM/r. C1: rearranged for r. C1: v in m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('b9e42bdf-ee83-5ae7-8ce6-32cc503e8fd6', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 45, 45, 'A satellite in a circular orbit has kinetic energy Ek. What is its gravitational potential energy?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '−2Ek', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Ek = ½mv² = GMm/2r and Ep = −GMm/r = −2Ek. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Ek = GMm/2r. C1: Ep = −GMm/r. A1: D (−2Ek).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cf731617-db25-5643-a096-09abb10e75f1', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 46, 46, 'The Hubble Space Telescope orbits 540 km above the Earth''s surface. Calculate its orbital speed and the time for one orbit. Give the time in minutes. Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '95.32', 'minutes', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'r = 6.37 × 10⁶ + 5.4 × 10⁵ = 6.91 × 10⁶ m. v = √(GM/r) = √(3.98 × 10¹⁴ ÷ 6.91 × 10⁶) = 7590 m s⁻¹. T = 2πr/v = 2π × 6.91 × 10⁶ ÷ 7591 = 5720 s. In minutes: 5719 ÷ 60 = 95.3. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: r = R + h. C1: v = √(GM/r). C1: T = 2πr/v. A1: T = 95.3 minutes.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('cc6d35ef-cdbf-5fc6-9655-0c5cc5465375', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 47, 47, 'Satellite X orbits a planet at radius r with speed v. Satellite Y orbits the same planet at radius 9r. What is the speed of Y?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'v/3', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v ∝ 1/√r: √9 = 3, so Y moves at v/3. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: v ∝ r^(−½). C1: √9. A1: B (v/3).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg2277cc32-t fg2277cc32-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg2277cc32-t">Two orbits</title><desc id="fg2277cc32-d">A planet with two circular orbits drawn to scale: satellite X at radius r and satellite Y at radius 9r.</desc><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="10" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="14" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="178.8" y="142.8" width="12" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="196.8" y="134.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">X: r</text><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="126" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><rect x="163" y="30.5" width="12" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="181" y="22.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Y: 9r</text></svg>', 'A planet with two circular orbits drawn to scale: satellite X at radius r and satellite Y at radius 9r.'),
('b9937454-3adc-5175-9fed-7a554cfb1db0', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 48, 48, 'Show that the orbital speed of a satellite just above the Earth''s surface (ignore air) is √(gR), and calculate it. g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹, R = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7905', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'mg = mv²/R, so v = √(gR) = √(9.81 × 6.37 × 10⁶) = 7910 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). M1: gravity = mv²/R. C1: substitution. A1: v = 7910 m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('0180f7b6-36ed-5a84-bdb5-4f43419edd63', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 49, 49, 'A satellite of mass 800 kg is in a circular orbit of radius 1.2 × 10⁷ m. Calculate its total energy (Ek + Ep = −GMm/2r). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and its radius = 6.37 × 10⁶ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '-1.327e10', 'J', false, NULL, true, NULL, 'E = −GMm/2r = −3.98 × 10¹⁴ × 800 ÷ (2 × 1.2 × 10⁷) = −1.33 × 10¹⁰ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = −GMm/2r. C1: substitution. A1: E = −1.33 × 10¹⁰ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('126369f7-39ee-5b17-98c6-1b42b71b7658', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 50, 50, 'A satellite in a circular orbit is slowed slightly by air resistance and drops into a lower circular orbit. What happens to its speed?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It increases', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'In the lower orbit v = √(GM/r) is larger. Air resistance removes energy, but the satellite loses twice as much Ep as it gains Ek, so its speed rises. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). C1: smaller r. C1: larger v. A1: C (It increases).', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg608f97f3-t fg608f97f3-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg608f97f3-t">Dropping to a lower orbit</title><desc id="fg608f97f3-d">A planet with the satellite''s original circular orbit (dashed) and the lower circular orbit it drops into (solid).</desc><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="63" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="100.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="251.2" y="91.2" width="12" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="269.2" y="83.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">lower orbit</text><circle cx="180" cy="160" r="126" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><rect x="227.2" y="41.8" width="12" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="245.2" y="33.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">original orbit</text></svg>', 'A planet with the satellite''s original circular orbit (dashed) and the lower circular orbit it drops into (solid).'),
('182f2047-230c-5e2f-875d-6e621a7e1a60', 'ac5e5832-2f40-4487-ba6b-19b47d21baac', '1585a36a-5b5c-5505-abe3-98e37e28c48b', 'a-level', '17.5', '9702 coursebook 17.5 · Orbiting under gravity', 51, 51, 'A planet orbits a star of mass 3.0 × 10³⁰ kg at 2.0 × 10¹¹ m. Calculate the distance it travels along its orbit in one Earth year (3.16 × 10⁷ s). Take G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.995e11', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(GM/r) = √(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 3.0 × 10³⁰ ÷ 2.0 × 10¹¹) = 3.16 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹. Distance = vt = 3.163 × 10⁴ × 3.16 × 10⁷ = 1.00 × 10¹² m. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: v = √(GM/r). A1: v. C1: distance = vt. A1: distance = 1.00 × 10¹² m.', 4, NULL, NULL)
